WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.720
Scarlett Johansson's lawsuit against Disney for&nbsp;
breach of contract is one of the hottest topics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.880
in the entertainment industry right now — and&nbsp;
it's not hard to understand why. There are many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.760
compelling dynamics at play. On the one hand, you&nbsp;
have a rich and successful movie star quibbling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.560
over the idea that $20 million plus streaming&nbsp;
royalties isn't "fair compensation." Conversely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.080
You have a major conglomerate, arguing that&nbsp;
they are trying to give fans a safer way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.880
watch the film amid another dangerous surge&nbsp;
of Covid-19. It's a complex legal issue.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.120
Regardless of which side of the fence you're on,&nbsp;
you probably have your own opinion on the subject.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.120
Well, you're not alone. Dave Bautista is&nbsp;
not only the first celebrity to respond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.360
to the spat between Johansson and Disney&nbsp;
— he's also a prominent figure in the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.160
And he had some very interesting&nbsp;
things to say on the matter.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.880
Dave Bautista has become a&nbsp;
popular figure in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.640
especially for his role as Drax the Destroyer&nbsp;
in the "Guardians of the Galaxy" movies — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.960
success of which has led to action turns such&nbsp;
as this year's "Army of the Dead" on Netflix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.960
He's also known for being quite outspoken&nbsp;
for a celebrity of his stature, and fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.720
were dismayed to learn back in May that he&nbsp;
won't be reprising his beloved character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.640
after the third and final "Guardians of the&nbsp;
Galaxy" movie, citing his reason for throwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.280
in the towel being that he is "expecting&nbsp;
everything to start sagging any second now."

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.320
In any case, the fact that Bautista is&nbsp;
getting ready to finish up his time in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.760
the MCU didn't stop him from throwing&nbsp;
in his two cents about this latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.480
Disney controversy on social media. His&nbsp;
tweet, punctuated by a shrug emoji, reads,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.520
"Told em they should've made&nbsp;
a #Drax movie but noooooo!"

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.600
Fans seem to be mixed on his attempt at&nbsp;
levity. As of this video, the tweet has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.600
over 20,000 likes and 1,700 retweets, but in the&nbsp;
replies, while many followers are chuckling at his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.920
tongue-in-cheek humor, there are also many&nbsp;
others who believe it to be in poor taste.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.960
I know what you’re thinking, “People overreacting&nbsp;
on Twitter? NO WAY!” But yes, it really happened.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.040
One commenter replied with, "Bad tweet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.200
Delete please." Another replied with&nbsp;
a little sarcasm of their own saying,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.040
"Yeah, because there is a huge lack of&nbsp;
male superhero movies. I know it's a joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.400
but it's a bad one, especially now that&nbsp;
Disney is trying to portray Scarlet as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.040
bad person when they clearly broke the contract."

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.800
There were even several chiming in pointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.560
to what they saw as sexism inherent in&nbsp;
the lawsuit, summing it up by tweeting:

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.880
"This tweet ain't it. Scarlett's contract was&nbsp;
breached period and she's more than entitled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.280
to the damages they've caused her. She spend a&nbsp;
decade creating one of the most iconic characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.440
suffered sexism and hyper sexualization&nbsp;
and now this crap. She deserved better."

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.320
Other Twitter users were more&nbsp;
supportive of Bautista's joke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.960
One expressed interest in a&nbsp;
potential Drax-centric film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.040
tweeting "I would have taken my butt to the&nbsp;
theater to see a Drax movie...repeatedly."

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.120
Another user, also backed this up with a serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.960
suggestion that instead of Drax getting&nbsp;
a movie, he should get a Disney+ series:

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.680
"Not a movie, but a tv show based on the&nbsp;
Infinity Watch comics. I believe it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.160
better fit for a Drax spinoff. If you can mr&nbsp;
Bautista, try to push for that with Disney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.520
sell it as one more MCU show to&nbsp;
promote their streaming service."

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.480
Either way, the situation playing&nbsp;
out between Johansson and Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.080
is certainly complicated — more than&nbsp;
just some jokes and reax on Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.640
can explain — and it may very well only get&nbsp;
more complicated as it plays out in court.

